    "title": "The Science of Habit Formation",
    "topic": "Psychology",
    "difficulty": "Band 6.5–7.0",
    "passage": "A\n\nHabits are repeated behaviours that become easier to perform over time. They allow people to complete familiar actions with less conscious effort. This efficiency is useful because attention is limited. If every routine action required careful thought, even ordinary life would become exhausting.\n\nB\n\nResearchers often describe habits as involving a cue, a behaviour, and a result. A cue may be a place, a time, an emotion, or the presence of another person. The behaviour follows, and the result may provide comfort, pleasure, or relief. Repetition strengthens the connection between these elements.\n\nC\n\nThis model is helpful, but real habits are not always simple. A person may check a phone because of boredom, anxiety, social expectation, or a notification sound. Different cues can lead to the same action, while the same cue may produce different responses.\n\nD\n\nThe time required to form a habit varies greatly. Popular claims sometimes suggest that twenty-one days is enough, but research does not support one universal number. Simple actions performed in stable situations may become automatic relatively quickly. More demanding behaviours may take much longer.\n\nE\n\nEnvironment often matters more than willpower. A person who keeps fruit in a visible place may eat it more often, while someone who stores a phone outside the bedroom may check it less at night. These changes reduce the need for repeated self-control.\n\nF\n\nBreaking an unwanted habit can be difficult because the original cue remains. Simply deciding to stop may not be enough. Replacing the old behaviour with a different response is often more effective than attempting to create an empty gap.\n\nG\n\nRewards also need to be considered carefully. Immediate rewards can strengthen a new routine, but external rewards may sometimes reduce internal motivation. If a person exercises only to earn points, the behaviour may disappear when the points are removed.\n\nH\n\nSocial context can support or weaken habits. People are more likely to continue a behaviour when it is normal within their family, workplace, or friendship group. Public commitments may help some individuals, but they can create pressure for others.\n\nI\n\nSleep, stress, and physical health also affect repetition. A person may follow a plan successfully during a calm week but return to an older routine during exhaustion or emotional difficulty. This does not necessarily mean the plan was useless; it may mean the environment changed.\n\nJ\n\nResearchers therefore caution against presenting habit change as a matter of personal discipline alone. Successful change often involves redesigning cues, reducing friction, selecting realistic behaviours, and accepting that progress may be uneven.\n\nK\n\nThe study of habits is still developing. Digital devices now allow researchers to collect detailed information about behaviour in daily life, but they also create privacy concerns. Future research may provide more individualised advice, although no method is likely to work equally well for everyone.",
