    "title": "Reconstructing Ancient Diets",
    "topic": "Archaeology",
    "difficulty": "Band 6.5–7.0",
    "passage": "A\n\nUnderstanding what ancient people ate can reveal far more than their preferred foods. Diet provides evidence about trade, social class, farming, migration, climate, and health. Yet reconstructing ancient meals is difficult because most food disappears quickly after use.\n\nB\n\nArchaeologists first relied heavily on visible remains such as animal bones, seeds, shells, and cooking pots. These materials remain important, but each has limits. Large animal bones survive better than soft plants, and foods cooked without durable containers may leave little evidence.\n\nC\n\nMicroscopic analysis has expanded the available evidence. Tiny starch grains can remain on stone tools, while plant crystals called phytoliths may survive in soil or dental deposits. These traces can identify foods that would otherwise vanish.\n\nD\n\nHuman teeth provide another source of information. Dental wear may indicate whether food was coarse or heavily processed. Cavities can suggest diets rich in certain carbohydrates, although health is influenced by many other factors.\n\nE\n\nChemical analysis of bones and teeth can also reveal long-term dietary patterns. Different foods contain distinctive ratios of stable isotopes. By comparing these ratios, researchers may estimate the relative importance of marine food, land animals, or particular crops.\n\nF\n\nThe method is powerful but not exact. Similar chemical signals can come from different foods, and local environmental conditions affect the results. Researchers therefore need comparative samples from plants, animals, and soil in the same region.\n\nG\n\nResidue analysis examines molecules preserved inside pottery. Fats from dairy products, meat, fish, or plants may remain for thousands of years. However, one pot may have been used for many foods, and later contamination can complicate interpretation.\n\nH\n\nWritten records can provide menus, tax lists, or trade accounts, but they often describe the diets of wealthy or literate groups. Ordinary meals may be underrepresented. Images of feasts can also be symbolic rather than realistic.\n\nI\n\nAnimal remains may reveal butchery methods, hunting preferences, and access to particular cuts of meat. Yet bones may have been moved by dogs, water, or later human activity. Their location must therefore be interpreted cautiously.\n\nJ\n\nThe strongest reconstructions combine multiple forms of evidence. Bones, plant remains, chemical data, pottery residues, and written sources can support or challenge one another. Agreement between independent methods increases confidence.\n\nK\n\nEven then, archaeologists avoid claiming certainty. Ancient diets changed with season, age, status, and location. A burial population may not represent everyone who lived in a settlement. Reconstructing food is therefore an exercise in probability rather than a complete recovery of the past.",
