    "difficulty": "Band 6.5–7.0",
    "passage": "A\n\nPublic spaces include streets, squares, parks, markets, and transport stations. They are places where people who do not necessarily know one another share the same environment. Their design influences movement, safety, commerce, and social contact.\n\nB\n\nHistorically, public squares often served several purposes at once. They hosted markets, political speeches, religious events, celebrations, and punishment. Their meaning changed depending on time and use.\n\nC\n\nModern planning sometimes separated these activities. Roads were designed for vehicles, shopping moved indoors, and residential districts became more private. This improved efficiency in some respects but reduced informal interaction.\n\nD\n\nIn recent years, many cities have attempted to make streets more social. Temporary seating, street markets, pedestrian zones, and outdoor performances can encourage people to remain rather than simply pass through.\n\nE\n\nSupporters argue that lively public space strengthens community. Repeated encounters with familiar strangers may create trust even when people never become close friends. Public space can also give visibility to groups that are otherwise ignored.\n\nF\n\nHowever, popularity can produce conflict. Residents may complain about noise, businesses may occupy too much space, and security measures may exclude young or homeless people. A place that appears welcoming to one group may feel controlled to another.\n\nG\n\nTechnology has changed public behaviour. People use phones for navigation, work, entertainment, and communication while moving through shared spaces. Digital connection can support coordination, but it may also reduce attention to the immediate surroundings.\n\nH\n\nCommercial pressure is another influence. Cafés, advertising, ticketed events, and private security can make a space attractive while also limiting who feels able to use it without spending money.\n\nI\n\nClimate is another important factor. Shade, shelter, water, and seating affect whether people can use a space comfortably. As heatwaves become more common, public areas designed without climate protection may become unusable for part of the year.\n\nJ\n\nSuccessful public spaces are rarely produced by design alone. Maintenance, transport access, local rules, and surrounding businesses all matter. Community participation can improve planning, although consultation does not guarantee agreement.\n\nK\n\nPublic space is therefore not simply empty land between buildings. It is a social system shaped by design, power, culture, and daily behaviour. Its value lies partly in allowing unexpected contact, but that openness must be managed carefully.",
