    "id": 1006,
    "title": "Why Scientific Replication Matters",
    "topic": "Research Methods",
    "difficulty": "Band 6.5–7.0",
    "passage": "A\n\nScientific knowledge is often presented as a collection of established facts, but individual studies can be wrong. A result may depend on chance, measurement error, unusual participants, or analytical choices. Replication is the process of repeating research to see whether a similar result can be obtained again.\n\nB\n\nA direct replication follows the original method as closely as possible. A conceptual replication tests the same idea using different procedures. Both are valuable. Direct replication checks reliability, while conceptual replication examines whether a finding applies beyond one specific design.\n\nC\n\nFor many years, replication received less attention than novel research. Journals preferred surprising findings, universities rewarded originality, and funding agencies often supported new projects rather than repeated ones. As a result, some influential claims were widely accepted before being tested carefully.\n\nD\n\nConcerns increased when large replication projects failed to reproduce a substantial number of published findings. This did not prove that all original studies were false. It did show that confidence had sometimes been too high.\n\nE\n\nSeveral factors may explain replication failure. The original result may have been a statistical accident. The replication may differ in an important way. Cultural or historical conditions may also change the effect being studied.\n\nF\n\nSmall sample sizes are another problem. When only a few participants are studied, estimates are less stable. A dramatic result may appear by chance and then become weaker in a larger group.\n\nG\n\nPublication bias can further distort the literature. Studies with positive or exciting findings are more likely to appear in journals than studies with negative results. This creates a record that may exaggerate the strength of evidence.\n\nH\n\nResearchers have proposed reforms. Preregistration requires scientists to record hypotheses and methods before collecting or analysing data. Open data allows others to inspect results. Registered reports are evaluated partly before the findings are known.\n\nI\n\nThese reforms are not perfect. Preregistration cannot prevent every flexible decision, and open data may create privacy or misuse concerns. Nevertheless, transparency makes hidden choices easier to identify.\n\nJ\n\nReplication may also reveal that an effect works only under particular conditions. This is not necessarily a failure. Narrower conclusions can be more accurate and useful than broad claims that do not survive further testing.\n\nK\n\nReplication should not be viewed as an attack on science. On the contrary, it is one of the methods by which science corrects itself. A field becomes stronger when it can distinguish findings that are robust from those that depend on narrow conditions or chance.",
